{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/yang-clojure/1795455124699-reviewer-p4-php-clojure-core.glm.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: 2026-11-24 08:55:00 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-24 15:55:00 +07\nReviewer-Model: glm-5.3\nReviewing: uncommitted working tree, branch `feat/p4-php-clojure-core`, base master `0c36043d`\nFiles: 8 (5 new read whole: contract/profiles/clj_runtime/descriptor_profile_test; 3 modified hunked: semantics, frontend, lower, repl/frontends)\n\n# Step-7 review - yang.preludes P4'-b (`:yang.php/clojure-core-v1`)\n\n**Overall: the slice is substantiated and design-conformant.** The dual-shape entry contract is implemented exactly per S6.4 item 6 (frozen `php/run-main` 4-parameter lambda - param names `[body request response-stream diag-stream]` verified against `prelude.cljc:5863` - legitimately called with 2 or 4 operands via the contract's recorded `:call-shapes #{2 4}` + `:operands` map, request stream in slot two), `yang.core.semantics` was generalized in the contract-recorded shape (map-operands branch in `callable!`) and not in the frozen prelude, and the 3-operand rejection is implemented (`contract/entry-operands` throws `:defect :arity`) and fixture-exercised (`descriptor_profile_test.cljc:55-57`).\n\nLegacy `php-v1` preservation reads clean: every legacy hunk is an `app* 'php/x` to `(hook ctx :php/x ...)` swap where `legacy-hooks` maps the role to the identical symbol; literal strategies reproduce the old nodes exactly (verified: `(u/sexp->uast :php/null)` is node-identical to `(u/lit :php/null)` for the request default; the forced `bits 64` in `builtin-constant-node` keeps positive builtin ints on `php/int-lit`; the `2**53` int boundary via bit-length 53 equals the old `max-safe-int` compare). The clj.php adapter has zero provenance doors (only `clj/make-callable`, `clj/invoke-callable`, `clj/prim-get`), mirrors the landed `clj.py` `_run`/policy pattern, and the stream-output gate matches native `php/run-main` (body record `stream/put!` only when output is non-empty and a response stream is attached; the fixture proves both cases in bundled+linked). The DEFAULT flip reuses the P3' mechanism with a backward-compatible generalization (`:yang.frontend/preferred` as map or pair), in-flight snapshots retained, bare compiler calls keep the native pin (`:or {descriptor legacy-descriptor}`). Capability honesty: all 27 declared capabilities trace to enforcement sites; `admit-key?` flows through `clj/collection-key`'s `:coll/unsupported-key` policy; reserved-door ban holds and is fixture-probed. Fences hold: no `yang/typescript/` changes, php prelude modules untouched, `:cljd/:jolt` portability gates, canonical ordering, boxed float paths throughout `clj.php`, and the cljs `1e15` postwalk is correctly `:cljs`-scoped.\n\n## Findings\n\n**MEDIUM | src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:3382-3384,3413-3423** | Closure captures leak into functions *declared* inside a closure body. `persistent-lambda` (3448) passes `(assoc ctx :captures slots)` down; when a `functionDeclaration` occurs inside that lambda body, `lower-function`'s non-legacy branch forwards that ctx to `persistent-function`, whose `names` includes `(keys (:captures ctx))` and whose binding init wires them to `%capture-*` slots. So `$f = function() use ($x) { function inner() { return $x; } };` binds `inner`'s `$x` to the closure's *captured* value, where PHP reads `inner`'s own scope (implicit null + notice - a declared function is never a closure). Silent wrong value in the default profile; only nested declared functions inside captured closures are affected (nested lambdas replace `:captures` correctly; plain function-in-function is unaffected since no `:captures` is in scope). Untested: all suite programs declare functions at top level. Fix: strip the leak at the boundary - `lower-function`'s non-legacy branch should call `(persistent-function (dissoc ctx :captures) ...)` (a declared function is not a closure) - and/or add a fixture for a function declared inside a closure.\n\n**MEDIUM | src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:813-824** | Bare `isset($x)` under `clojure-core-v1` fails closed through a *native* hook with a misleading reason. `isset-var` was never given a persistent branch: inside a function it emits `:php/local-isset?` (823), at top level `scope-op \"isset?\"` maps to `:php/global-isset?` (824); both roles are native-only, so `hook-name`'s fallback refuses with `:capability :runtime/native :construct :php/global-isset?`. The surface is inconsistent: `isset($a['k'])` *works* (offset path lowers to a null-compare at 1090-1093), `empty($x)` works (1183-1187 lowers to `not(truthy(peek))`), and `lower-call-plain`'s non-legacy `case` special-cases `\"isset\"` (1979ff) as if supported. Nothing in `clojure-capabilities` names this refusal, and the capability-refusal fixture list has no isset entry, so the refusal is undeclared and untested. (Related known narrowing, acceptable per profile: the offset form's null-compare conflates a stored `null` value with an absent key - `isset($m[null])` on `[null => 'nil']` answers false.) Fix: either lower bare `isset` under the persistent profile to a presence check the profile can express (`(not (= <read> nil))`, consistent with `empty`'s treatment, or a `data/contains?`-backed helper over the state's `:vars`), or declare the refusal honestly (dedicated capability + fixture) instead of blaming `:runtime/native`.\n\n**LOW | src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:1987** | `lower-call-plain`'s non-legacy `\"unset\"` arm returns the result of `require-capability!` - nil - when the capability is *not* refused, silently lowering `unset(...)` to nothing. Currently unreachable (`:mutation/delete` is `:refused` in `clojure-capabilities`, and legacy traffic takes `lower-native-call`), but a future descriptor admitting `:mutation/delete` would silently drop the call instead of refusing or implementing it. Fix: after the capability check, `(unsupported! n :unset)` (or lower it to the statement form) so the arm never returns nil.\n\n**NOTE | src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/contract.cljc:18-21** | `==` (`:op/eq`) and `===` (`:op/identical`) both map to `clj.php/eq` (`data/equiv`), and `!=`/`!==` both to `ne`: loose equality collapses onto kind-strict equality under the profile. Consistent with the P3' python family and the truth-in-advertising stance, and demonstrated by fixtures (`1 == 1.0` false under the profile vs `\"1\"` under legacy; `[NAN] == [NAN]` false), but unlike python (where `is`/`is-not` were explicitly legacy-only roles) the design doc's php contract does not spell this narrowing out. Worth one line in `docs/design/yang.php.md`'s profile table when P4'-b is recorded.\n\n**NOTE | OPEN items** | Neither blocks approval. (1) The wide JVM e2e run lacking final counts is an evidence gap on the slow tier, not a defect: the legacy emission is symbol- and node-identical by construction (verified by reading; corroborated by lower-portable+prelude 41/1627/0 and the captured 1,325-fixture zero-diff parity incl. 518 permutations + 271 PHPT). (2) The absent full-permutation oracle file is the shiva-recorded exhaustive corpus (`test/yang/php/antlr/permutations.cljc` header); the 518-permutation tier that *was* run plus the PHPT suite already cover the refcount/reference machinery the refactor could have disturbed. Recommendation: complete the wide e2e run before merging this branch to master.\n\n**NOTE | src/cljc/yin/repl/frontends.cljc:114-116** | The clojure-profile `:render` uses host `pr-str` on the uncaught guest value; multi-key map printing order is host-unspecified (display-only - nothing recorded in artifacts or goldens; the uncaught-render path is also untested). If REPL-display parity across hosts ever matters, route through the guest `str`.\n\n## Focus-item verification summary\n\n1. **Dual-shape entry contract** - verified end-to-end: `entry-lowering` records both shapes with exact frozen param names; `semantics/callable!`'s new map branch (semantics.cljc:194-199) checks shapes = operand-map keys and slot counts, then arity/params against the export's verified signature; 2- and 4-operand emission goes through `contract/entry-operands`, which rejects 3 (`:defect :arity :call-shape 3`, fixture at test:55-57); role-substitution refusals (test:46-51) exercise the r8 authorization-key gate. The generalization lives in the contract/semantics layer; the frozen prelude is untouched.\n2. **Legacy `php-v1` preservation** - lower.cljc hunks are descriptor reads plus legacy-guarded no-ops (`with-temps`, `acquired`, `typed-inc`, addref); duplicate-declare against `prelude/builtin-names` is legacy-only (deliberate: profile functions live in the state cell and shadow projections - fixture `function count(...)`); request/response defaults emit identical literal nodes. See NOTE on the OPEN e2e item.\n3. **clj.php adapter** - no provenance doors; `:fn/make` to `clj/make-callable` (arity-checking wrapper with the `{:required ...}` spec), `:fn/call` to `clj/invoke-callable`; `run-main` installs `:on-unhandled-policy clj.php/_policy` on `(clj/rt-ctx)` inside a save/restore `try-finally`, exactly the python `_run` pattern; stream gating condition matches native (non-empty output AND attached stream), and the 2-operand shape returns the accumulated record list.\n4. **DEFAULT flip** - `standard` installs both revisions and prefers `clojure-core-v1` per-language via the map form; `select` accepts map or pair (pair catalogs behave exactly as before); explicit `\"php-v1\"` pin selects the legacy manifest (fixture asserts its revision and `1 == 1.0` giving `\"1\"`); in-flight state keeps its snapshot (fixture re-evaluates after the catalog switch); bare `lower-packet` keeps `legacy-descriptor`.\n5. **Descriptor honesty** - every declared capability traced to an enforcement site; `legacy-capabilities` all-`:supported` with `:mutation/locals :cell` matches the frozen surface; missing-hook logic correctly permits `:io/echo`/`:unit/init` (native-gated) to be absent from the clojure hooks and the persistent roles to be absent from legacy; reserved doors banned and probed.\n6. **Fences + portability** - no ts/php-prelude changes; `#?(:cljd/:jolt)` gates in requires and install; canonical-compare ordering in resolver/captures; boxed float discipline in `clj.php` numerics (`to-float-boxed`, `true-div-boxed`, `compare-float-boxed`, `fpow-half`, `neg-pow`); the cljs float-restore postwalk is the only host patch and is correctly `:cljs`-scoped.\n\nVERDICT: APPROVED-WITH-FIXES\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md'","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
